    {
      "description": "Monarchy is a governmental form organized around a continuing monarchic office. Succession, formal authority, practical power, sacred status, and democratic accountability vary independently.",
      "id": "monarchy-dossier",
      "kind": "dossier",
      "label": "Monarchy dossier",
      "publicationStatus": "reviewed",
      "reviewedAt": "2026-09-06",
      "sections": [
        {
          "body": "The office is not the officeholder, and monarchy is not the same thing as monarchism, a dynasty's tradition, a government's self-description, or an external regime classification.
A monarch may be head of state without being head of government.
Constitutional and parliamentary monarchy describes an allocation of offices and responsibility; it does not by itself prove democracy or a powerless sovereign.
Executive dynastic monarchy describes a different configuration, but it also should not be reduced to one person's rule.",
          "heading": "What can monarchy mean?",
          "id": "meanings",
          "relatedEntityRefs": [
            {
              "id": "monarchism",
              "kind": "concept",
            },
            {
              "id": "constitutional-parliamentary-monarchy",
              "kind": "approach",
            },
            {
              "id": "executive-dynastic-monarchy",
              "kind": "approach",
            },
          ],
          "statementIds": [
