        {
          "body": "Saudi Arabia's 1992 Basic Law declares a monarchy, confines succession to the founder's descendants through an allegiance and designation process, and makes the King head of government.
It grounds governmental authority in al-Qurʾān (القرآن, Qur'an) and al-Sunnah (السنة, Sunnah).
Its Article 56 says the King shall be prime minister, while a September 2022 royal order appointed Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman to that office as an express exception.
Herb supplies a bounded analytical definition of dynastic monarchy rather than evidence about every current Saudi office.
Those facts support analysis of religious legal authority and dynastic government; neither “theocracy” nor “authoritarianism” follows from the crown without a separate definition and institutional evidence.",
          "heading": "How does Saudi Arabia allocate authority?",
          "id": "saudi-arabia",
          "relatedEntityRefs": [
            {
              "id": "saudi-basic-law-monarchy-1992-2022",
              "kind": "case",
            },
          ],
          "statementIds": [
            "saudi-monarchy-basic-law",
            "saudi-succession-designation",
            "saudi-crown-prince-designation",
            "saudi-basic-law-king-prime-minister-clause",
            "saudi-2022-crown-prince-prime-minister",
            "saudi-religious-law-rule",
            "saudi-theocracy-classification-boundary",
            "saudi-dynastic-rules-in-use",
            "saudi-ruling-family-institution-herb",
          ],
          "traceStatus": "qualified",
        },
        {
          "body": "Japan, Tonga, and Saudi Arabia are bounded contrasts drawn from evidence with different cutoffs, not embodiments of monarchy or claims of present-day completeness.
Empire concerns control over dependent territory, colonial rule concerns domination of one people by another, and nobility names a titled social group; none is synonymous with a monarchic head-of-state office.
Each comparison must state which office, succession rule, formal authority, rule in use, and accountability relationship it is comparing.",
          "heading": "Which neighboring labels should remain separate?",
          "id": "boundaries",
          "statementIds": [
            "monarchy-theocracy-boundary",
            "monarchy-republic-boundary",
            "monarchy-empire-boundary",
            "monarchy-colonial-rule-boundary",
            "monarchy-nobility-boundary",
            "three-cases-nonrepresentative",
          ],
          "traceStatus": "qualified",
        },
      ],
      "standfirst": "Monarchy is a governmental form organized around a continuing monarchic office, but the crown alone does not tell us who succeeds, who governs, or who can hold power to account.
Hereditary, elective, rotational, designated, and disputed succession must be distinguished, as must an office's legal powers, delegated acts, reserve powers, civic functions, and influence in practice.",
      "standfirstStatementIds": [
        "monarchy-office-form-definition",
        "monarchy-succession-varies",
        "monarchy-democracy-boundary",
      ],
      "subject": {
        "id": "monarchy",
        "kind": "concept",
      },
    },
